# Debugging Missing Authorization Headers in Laravel

When integrating an API client like Postman to test endpoints, it is common to expect that headers sent via `Authorization: Bearer <token>` are immediately accessible within your application logic. However, a frequent stumbling block occurs when the header appears valid in the client but resolves to `NULL` inside the controller or middleware. This discrepancy often stems from how Laravel parses incoming requests and where specific header values are actually stored before they reach your `Request` object.

The standard approach to retrieve headers in Laravel is using the `Request::header()` helper. If you attempt to access this directly without verifying the request lifecycle, you might encounter unexpected null returns even when Postman clearly transmits the data. To diagnose this, developers often resort to dumping the entire header collection, as seen in the following snippet:

```php
die(var_dump(Request::header()));
```

In many scenarios involving API authentication, this dump reveals that only standard headers like `Host`, `Cookie`, and occasionally `Content-Type` are present. The `Authorization` header is conspicuously absent from the output. This behavior indicates that Laravel has not yet processed the incoming request into its full header collection because the parsing phase has either not completed or the specific header was stripped at a different layer of the stack.

The root cause often lies in the distinction between raw headers and parsed headers within the context of routing. In older versions of Laravel or specific configurations, accessing `Request::header('Authorization')` immediately after a route definition might return null if the request hasn't been fully initialized. However, modern Laravel applications typically handle this seamlessly during the controller execution phase. If you are seeing `NULL`, it is highly probable that the application is not using the global Request instance correctly or is accessing headers before the middleware responsible for parsing them has executed.

Another critical consideration involves how Postman sends the request versus how the server receives it. When using Postman to send a GET request with an Authorization header, ensure that you are actually sending the raw header and not relying on an OAuth2 tab setting that might serialize the token differently depending on the HTTP method or content type. For example, if you set up a POST request in Postman but accidentally use `Content-Type: application/json`, some configurations might interfere with how certain proxies or gateways forward headers.

To retrieve the header reliably within a controller or middleware, always ensure you are accessing the property after the request has entered your defined routes and any configured middleware has run. A more robust pattern involves using the `$request` instance injected into your controller methods rather than relying on the static helper in every instance:

```php
class UserController extends Controller
{
    public function show(User $user)
    {
        // Accessing the header from the request instance
        $authHeader = $this->request->header('Authorization');

        if (!$authHeader) {
            abort(401, 'Unauthenticated.');
        }

        // Proceed with logic
    }
}
```

This approach ensures that the `$this->request` property reflects the fully parsed state of the incoming HTTP request. If you are building a middleware to handle authentication globally, accessing `$request->header('Authorization')` inside the `handle()` method should yield the expected Bearer token without issues.

It is also worth noting that if your application runs behind a load balancer or reverse proxy like Nginx or Apache, those layers might strip or modify headers before they reach the PHP-FPM process. In such cases, the header might not appear in your Laravel dump regardless of what Postman sends. Always verify your server configuration to ensure headers are forwarded correctly through the proxy chain.

For production environments, relying solely on `Request::header()` without validation can lead to subtle security gaps. If you need to validate the token format immediately upon receipt, consider using the `auth:sanctum` or `throttle` middleware groups which handle header parsing and validation out of the box. These tools abstract away the complexity of raw header access and provide standardized responses for missing or invalid credentials.

Ultimately, the issue of a missing `Authorization` header usually resolves to ensuring that the request has been fully processed by Laravel's internal stack before attempting to read the value. By inspecting the request lifecycle and verifying proxy configurations, you can reliably access authentication headers and secure your API endpoints effectively.
